  "verbalization": "Etodolac, a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID), is consistently linked to Cyp2c9 through its inclusion in the Nonsteroidal Anti-inflammatory Compounds class, which includes oxaprozin, meloxicam, and mefenamic acid. All three compounds bind to Cyp2c9, suggesting a shared mechanism of action involving the cytochrome P450 2C9 enzyme. The lowest common ancestors of Etodolac and these NSAIDs—Monocarboxylic Acid and Organic Heteropolycyclic Compound—highlight a common biochemical pathway, reinforcing the notion that Etodolac exerts its effects via a shared metabolic route. The evidence suggests that Etodolac may inhibit Cyp2c9, potentially affecting drug metabolism, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains to be fully elucidated. The consistent presence of NSAIDs in the same class and their binding to Cyp2c9 supports a mechanistic link, but further experimental validation is needed to confirm the specific mode of action and clinical relevance.",
